00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:04.440
Neil: I'm Neil Cox.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.880
I am a consultant within
Capita Consulting.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.190
A consultant doing lots of
projects and program management.

00:00:14.684 --> 00:00:18.134
I've had a variety of different
roles throughout my career.

00:00:18.314 --> 00:00:22.185
Project management world it's really
good to get close to the outputs

00:00:22.185 --> 00:00:23.384
that you're looking to deliver.

00:00:23.454 --> 00:00:27.594
You know, it really is exciting just
to see that change and transformation

00:00:27.624 --> 00:00:32.094
piece of work just, just going from
an idea at the start to whatever

00:00:32.094 --> 00:00:33.744
you have to deliver afterwards.

00:00:33.808 --> 00:00:37.703
But as you get more and more complex
through your work, being able

00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:41.843
to do some more bigger strategic
roles are also so valuable because

00:00:41.843 --> 00:00:43.463
you'll focus more on outcomes.

00:00:43.793 --> 00:00:47.483
And how can you make a business
change or, or really drive outcomes

00:00:47.543 --> 00:00:49.883
into a community or a piece of work.

00:00:50.203 --> 00:00:56.093
Yeah, I've been a project manager
for around about 18 years.

00:00:56.123 --> 00:01:01.973
What is really good is projects can
stem from being very short and snappy

00:01:02.123 --> 00:01:05.713
you know even a few days -- spending
a week on something -- to some

00:01:05.738 --> 00:01:08.768
other projects, which are much more
complex and they take much longer.

00:01:09.278 --> 00:01:11.198
But what that gives you is variety.

00:01:11.348 --> 00:01:16.448
My key responsibilities as a project
manager have to be running that

00:01:16.448 --> 00:01:18.518
project on a day-to-day basis.

00:01:19.448 --> 00:01:22.928
So we'll have a project board, or
we will have a senior responsible

00:01:22.928 --> 00:01:24.578
owner or program manager.

00:01:24.638 --> 00:01:28.958
They'll be turning to you to
say, can you run this project?

00:01:29.708 --> 00:01:33.508
And you then need to own it from
cradle to grave from start to finish.

00:01:33.898 --> 00:01:37.773
There will always be people on hand to
help with questions or pointers if you're

00:01:37.773 --> 00:01:42.993
unsure with things, but it's yours and
you need to run with it and operate it.

00:01:43.473 --> 00:01:48.333
And that goes through all of the
aspects of it, all of the steps

00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:49.783
in the entire project cycle.

00:01:49.783 --> 00:01:54.540
Or if you're using Prince 2 or Agile,
which whichever model that you're using,

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:59.370
you've got an opportunity then took to
own each one of those steps going through.

00:02:03.035 --> 00:02:08.315
This day and age, you can't afford to
have -- depending on how big the project

00:02:08.315 --> 00:02:14.255
is -- a dedicated project manager who
just manages the compliance, the risks,

00:02:14.255 --> 00:02:16.565
the plans, the finances, et cetera.

00:02:16.985 --> 00:02:19.145
Often you've got to get stuck in as well.

00:02:19.355 --> 00:02:23.255
So be able to lead from the
front and be able to roll up your

00:02:23.255 --> 00:02:26.315
sleeves, get involved with the
deliverables that need to take place.

00:02:26.324 --> 00:02:30.965
I myself, you know, whether it's writing,
doing business requirements, running

00:02:30.965 --> 00:02:38.517
workshops, to writing communications, to
stakeholder engagement, to doing warranty

00:02:38.517 --> 00:02:42.987
calls or, or health check calls, you
end up getting involved with everything.

00:02:46.092 --> 00:02:51.822
On a day-to-day basis, your role is
going to be, it's going to be different

00:02:51.852 --> 00:02:54.192
during different phases of a project.

00:02:54.642 --> 00:02:58.572
So if you focused just really on the
startup or the initiation part of a

00:02:58.572 --> 00:03:02.962
project, you're going to be working
through planning, scheduling, what's

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:04.302
the potential risks and issues.

00:03:04.867 --> 00:03:06.637
You can be looking into building a team.

00:03:06.907 --> 00:03:09.097
You can be looking into
writing business cases.

00:03:09.157 --> 00:03:11.797
You're going to be looking into
what's your project finances.

00:03:12.307 --> 00:03:17.317
And all of those are really key outputs
to make sure the project is set up

00:03:17.347 --> 00:03:20.257
on the right foot to go forwards.

00:03:21.942 --> 00:03:25.902
Once you've got everything up and running,
you'll then move into that delivery phase.

00:03:26.262 --> 00:03:30.432
And this is really around getting stuck
in and driving the project forwards

00:03:30.792 --> 00:03:36.282
and making sure that that all of those
project activities take place on time,

00:03:37.062 --> 00:03:39.432
on cost and to the right quality.

00:03:40.767 --> 00:03:46.707
You'll move to the closure phase, which
is very keen to make sure the work

00:03:46.707 --> 00:03:50.877
that you've done gets transitioned
very successfully over to the business

00:03:50.877 --> 00:03:54.477
as usual, or to whoever's going to
own that project going forwards.

00:03:54.513 --> 00:03:58.713
But whilst undertaking all of
those different steps and stages.

00:03:59.508 --> 00:04:03.078
You need to make sure you're still
on top of your plans, your risks,

00:04:03.078 --> 00:04:07.638
your issues, dependencies, financial
management reporting, and very

00:04:07.638 --> 00:04:09.858
critically stakeholder engagement.

00:04:14.268 --> 00:04:18.378
How do I work out how to assign
the right roles in my team?

00:04:18.618 --> 00:04:20.778
It's a real challenge.

00:04:21.468 --> 00:04:25.978
The first step you need to consider
is know what it is you're delivering.

00:04:26.798 --> 00:04:28.158
It's not a trick question.

00:04:28.188 --> 00:04:30.468
You need to be able to know
your strengths and weaknesses.

00:04:30.918 --> 00:04:34.938
During a planning phase at the
start of a project it's key to

00:04:34.938 --> 00:04:38.827
bring in experts at the right
point to help with your planning.

00:04:38.853 --> 00:04:43.113
There's no point planning and
scoping a, you know, a Microsoft

00:04:43.143 --> 00:04:47.283
365 migration or technical project,
if you don't actually know what's

00:04:47.283 --> 00:04:49.143
involved at each step along the way.

00:04:49.923 --> 00:04:53.748
So, it's quite key, you bring
in an expert when you need

00:04:53.748 --> 00:04:55.068
it during the planning phase.

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:59.748
And through that, you can work out
what are all the steps, all the tasks

00:04:59.748 --> 00:05:03.738
that you need to deliver throughout
your project and pulling on that

00:05:03.768 --> 00:05:06.528
expert, they'll be able to fill
in the blanks that you don't know.

00:05:08.108 --> 00:05:12.678
Then, once you've identified all of
those tasks and hopefully with a bit of

00:05:12.678 --> 00:05:17.418
your expert around, if you need them,
then you can work out what the right

00:05:17.418 --> 00:05:23.388
skill sets to help meet those tasks and
those requirements, you know, sometimes

00:05:23.388 --> 00:05:27.738
that can be quite challenging to find
resources to allocate to your project.

00:05:28.128 --> 00:05:32.358
And you will end up with the people that
might just be available and then you

00:05:32.358 --> 00:05:35.598
need to match up skillset to expertise.

00:05:39.738 --> 00:05:42.468
So what is the most important
part of the project lifecycle?

00:05:42.558 --> 00:05:45.658
I would say it has to be planning.

00:05:46.158 --> 00:05:51.288
It's definitely planning because you
want to make sure that your plan, your

00:05:51.288 --> 00:05:53.058
project is going to be successful.

00:05:53.928 --> 00:05:58.368
And we do a lot of work upfront
before launching a project

00:05:58.398 --> 00:06:00.648
to work out what do we need?

00:06:00.678 --> 00:06:01.758
When do we need it?

00:06:01.818 --> 00:06:02.718
Who do we need?

00:06:03.438 --> 00:06:04.428
What do we have to do?

00:06:04.488 --> 00:06:06.018
And why are we doing this?

00:06:06.438 --> 00:06:12.410
Answering all of those questions upfront
and testing it, validating it, checking

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:17.720
it with peers as well, is really key to
make sure you don't make mistakes later.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:23.280
You know, if you've got a carpenter coming
in, they will measure twice and cut once.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:27.080
It's much the same, do the
planning, do it right, check your

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.620
planning and then look to engage.

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:34.000
Many times I've been on projects
where the planning hasn't quite

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.470
been done to the right level.

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:40.630
And I found myself having to change
and adapt the project later on,

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.940
which might push it over budget.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:47.460
It might make it delayed or it might
just reduce the quality, but if you can't

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:51.570
address all of those items upfront, it
should save you a lot of pain later on.

